{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems","id":1560716,"title":"Dangers to Vampires - New Systems","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-06-18T13:16:31-07:00","id":1560716,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon1.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Dangers to Vampires - New Systems","content":"I'm looking at V5 Core pp 221 and trying not to die of disgust and sadness.<br /> <br /> The worse part, and reason I'm not marking this as [V5], is that the bulk of the problem isn't new to V5, it's old staples of VtM that should have been killed with prejudice 3 decades ago.<br /> <br /> So, Five entries total in my ramblings. Each will start with a problem, followed by a long wall of me dishing out frustration, and then something I hope to be a solution that helps people without the need to actually read or agree with the frustration. So: Section -&gt; Diagnosis -&gt; Fury -&gt; Proposal. Suggest all to skip Diagnosis and Fury unless wanting to engage in pointless debate. The proposals should be good even if you find RAW fine.<br /> <br /> Overall philosophy: my proposals focus on identifying how each rule affect the game and give the ST an informed choice, not to just say I know better than the book.<br /> <br /> Except Extreme Cold, that was just ridiculous.<br /> <br /> INDEX<br /> - Sunlight and Daysleep<br /> - Fire and Application<br /> - Decapitation and Metaphysics<br /> - Wooden stakes and Combat<br /> - Cold and Extreme Threats<br /> <br /> Sunlight and Daysleep<br /> <br /> The problem: the system tries to cover three distinct takes on it, but ends up favoring one so much it drowns the others.<br /> <br /> 1. Sunlight is dangerous and Daysleep is incapacitating. Kindred society simply can't afford to not be exclusively nocturnal.<br /> <br /> 2. Damnation adds nuance, not all monsters are equally at danger. Those closer to mortals can afford a longer stay.<br /> <br /> 3. Indirect exposure matters. You can be tactical about it, you can take calculated risks.<br /> <br /> But the actual damage rate is so brutal it just flattens everything to &quot;sunlight kills, don't go&quot;, the many details become mostly irrelevant and few tables ever put the rules to the test. There's no point. The rules could just be &quot;a vampire exposed to sunlight dies in 3 turns if don't get out&quot;. Staying awake is a sequence of boring, pointless rolls to still work capped.<br /> <br /> PROPOSAL<br /> <br /> - Main Rule (simple)<br /> A vampire suffer 2 Agg dmg each turn exposed to sunlight. The ST is the final judge on what counts as exposure, but there's no middle ground.<br /> Keep rules for awakening, but if damage is taken it's instant success for the scene to stay awake.<br /> REASONING: this is already how it works in practice, as the factors that change damage aren't enough to make it less urgent to escape. Take agg per turn, in a couple minutes tops you're toast.<br /> <br /> - The Mercy (optional rule)<br /> The purifying light of the sun punishes the Beast, not the Human beneath. If being moral protects against the sun, the rate of damage is as follows:<br /> - Humanity 0: 5/turn, no Fortitude applies<br /> - A Wight has no place under the sun, ever<br /> - Humanity 1-2: 2/turn<br /> - Regular, pretty harsh, run or perish<br /> - Humanity 3-4: 1/2 turns<br /> - Mild? This is still Agg, but you can brave a short run without it being a death sentence<br /> - Humanity 5-6: 1/scene<br /> - Still free Agg, but once in a while you can make a painful sacrifice to take care of business<br /> - Humanity 7-8: 1/day<br /> - Don't get confident, this will burn through your reserves (and skin) fast if you abuse, but you can actually function during day despite the smell of bacon<br /> - Humanity 9-10: Superficial/Daywalker<br /> - Whatever it is that a saint does during the day, be my guest<br /> REASONING: Aggravated damage is a big problem regardless of amount, most vamps won't start wandering by day just because they can survive the burning pain. But that does make for a setting that highlights Humanity vs the Beast, and what the later take away from you.<br /> <br /> - The Aura (optional rule)<br /> The stronger the Curse, the harder to escape judgement by the sun itself. If proximity to Caine is relevant in the matter, use an Exposure Score:<br /> - Exposure 10: summer clothes, sun high in a cloudless sky.<br /> - Exposure 0: total darkness, absolutely no visibility.<br /> - any layer of protection, from heavy clothes to covers or shadows, reduce Exposure, but...<br /> - add Bane Severity to Exposure.<br /> - define a Threshold (default 5), if Exposure \u2265 Threshold the character is exposed to sunlight.<br /> - Idea is solid, but numbers need tweaks. Take notes of what you try, what works, what don't.<br /> REASONING: this makes Bane Severity affect the damage in a way that actually matters as it gives you strategic decisions rather than slightly slower or faster certain destruction. It also adds to Kindred society a calculated benefit for the Embrace, as Neonates can more easily navigate the day even if everyone prefers the night still.<br /> <br /> For now that's what I can focus enough to make. I have ideas to hammer those and for the others, and would like the feedback.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind the important part is the structure, the precise numbers are just dials.<br /> <br /> <br /> Fire and Application<br /> <br /> The problem: as a recurring theme, the game never settled for real if it is about realistic combat, overly dramatic combat, tactical combat or cinematic combat. Trying to mix is valid, but means making decisions on how conflicting premises will resolve.<br /> <br /> Instead WoD mostly chose to not choose and the rules are all over the place without much attention to how they tell stories, causing not versatility, but incoherence.<br /> <br /> And few places felt this incoherence more than fire rules (or lack thereof), which is particularly egregious when it is the #1 method of causing Agg to everything that moves and especially pains vampires through R\u00f6tschreck.<br /> <br /> You see the results in the many, many, many discussions about how using fire is OP, fire powers are OP, fire this and that, despite fire IRL being at the same time everything the game says, yet most definitely not a common weapon in real conflicts.<br /> <br /> Why fire is a big deal?<br /> <br /> 1. It bites deep, no way around it. Any rule that tries to &quot;balance&quot; things by making fire cause less damage is missing the memo of why firefighters are firefighters, not just &quot;rescuers&quot;.<br /> <br /> 2. Fire is almost uncontrollable by nature. Yes, the Pyro is a fun unit in Team Fortress 2. IRL no one uses flamethrowers as weapons because it's stupid: they require extreme care and attention, are horrible for fine control and &quot;aim&quot;, and the user is basically stuck in place without being able to react to an actual combat as they are instead focused on applying fire without dying in the process, preferably also not destroying everything else than their target.<br /> <br /> 3. Fire isn't subtle. You don't cover up a fire, at most you cover up with fire. It causes a mess and leaves a mess. You can certainly destroy evidence with fire, but it's an admission that you destroyed evidence in the first place. Trying to quietly off vampires with fire is like putting silencers on frag grenades.<br /> <br /> 4. Fire is a known, constant danger. Our houses and apartments are built with fire safety protocols almost whenever you live in the world. Everything that uses fire is also full of warnings and safety instructions. And fire still causes accidents all the time, everywhere.<br /> <br /> 5. With all that, fire is easy to produce. You see discussions about how a given fire power is broken, when the PC could simply dump gas on something and put it on fire for same or worse results.<br /> <br /> 6. IRL fire-based weapons and ammo simply aren't what the hype says because they either:<br /> - Don't put anything on fire. Most phosphorus bullets and similar are just tracers without any remaining fire on them when hit target (and frequently less lethal because of deformation)<br /> - Work, and get all the same problems in the process. Dragon Breath ammo is an excellent example of a casing that robs your gun from all the benefits of being a gun and make it work as a glorified flamethrower. It has good technical reasons to exist, but they put it back squarely in the &quot;sensitive tool&quot; category of regular flamethrowers.<br /> - serve specific tactical purposes instead of being general use weapons, usually when the lack of control is the goal. Molotovs aren't for targeted destruction. They're for starting fires.<br /> - only exist for show. A lot of very cool weapons are actually bad at combat, but good at demonstration.<br /> <br /> Characters that use fire as weapons in media usually subtly cancel everything out by skill or fiat. What makes them cool is that they use fire, but what makes their use of fire cool is that they avoid all the problems. Is that the best approach for VtM?<br /> <br /> PROPOSAL<br /> <br /> - Control Fire Action (Wits + Resolve)<br /> Whenever you want to use, feed, start or put down fire, this is a Control action, no matter how big or small, no matter your purpose. It doesn't always require a roll, but it is always its own thing with its own time.<br /> - Crucially, that means you can't make attack or defensive rolls with fire, period. You can use it in combat, but only as a Control Fire Action. In V5 that means you don't participate in the regular combat contested roll, you forfeit.<br /> - Mechanically it's a decision tree where you start small and slow, then &quot;buy&quot; your outcome out of this paying with the risk of a fizzle or a backfire.<br /> <br /> Step 1: state your goal. Lighting a cigarette is a goal, putting the building on fire is a goal. Throwing a match in a tank of gasoline is not a goal, the goal is if you want it to catch fire or fizzle.<br /> Step 2: choose between Realistic and Dramatic fire.<br /> - Realistic is slow and safe, and has to be. Any resistance, hindrance or failure at planning means you back down. If everything is ok, no roll, you just do it.<br /> - Dramatic is risky, but is faster and don't back down. Roll Fire Control, if you fail, the fire gets out of control and you're probably the first/next to get a taste of it.<br /> - You can add Tactical rolls to plan ahead how the fire will spread, or Cinematic uses where the fire glows bright, but most of the heat is lost.<br /> <br /> Open to discussion, but that's the gist of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  Adding the Fire part. 06-25-2026, 08:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-18T13:46:54-07:00","id":1560719,"title":"","content":"I have to admit, since V5 was cribbing from Requiem so much anyway, I have no idea why they didn't copy VtR 2e's sunlight damage rules. You can port them over with almost zero effort (well, the damage numbers might be a bit high but not that much). Humanity dictates how much damage and what kind, Blood Potency dictates how often you take damage. Sufficient precautions reduce your effective BP by 1. Supernatural damage mitigation doesn't apply.<br /> <br /> IME it better balances the three main &quot;takes&quot; well, and reinforces the stratification of vampire society. Ghouls, thin-bloods, and fresh vampires can operate with relative ease during the day and thus have a significant place in vampire society by being able to do so. As vampires get older and stronger, their grip on Humanity slips, and they're forced more and more into the night. The lords of vampire society are powerful enough to demand whatever they want, but they can only really exercise that direct power when the sun is down; thus they have to be judicious and built up a vampire society that keeps them at the top, and forces conflict into more indirect arenas so younger vampires don't get ideas about burning down Havens during the day.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-18T16:45:54-07:00","id":1560729,"title":"","content":"My initial idea was to do precisely that, because yeah. It got a good balance overall and creates niches for Neonates that enriched the setting a lot.<br /> <br /> Instead they ignored this, but embraced Blood Potency that is redundant with Generation, and had to make a table of &quot;Generation within Generation&quot; and overall I find it so ugly. There were more elegant ways to have both concepts without making them a weird rule matryoshka.<br /> <br /> Since VtR2nd already have it, though, I decided to make my own version because I wanted something a little stronger. Also to have something that works if the ST decides that only BP matters, or only Humanity matters.<br /> <br /> Fire and Application<br /> <br /> The problem: as a recurring theme, the game never settled for real if it is about realistic combat, overly dramatic combat, tactical combat or cinematic combat. Trying to mix is valid, but means making decisions on how conflicting premises will resolve.<br /> <br /> Instead WoD mostly chose to not choose and the rules are all over the place without much attention to how they tell stories, causing not versatility, but incoherence.<br /> <br /> And few places felt this incoherence more than fire rules (or lack thereof), which is particularly egregious when it is the #1 method of causing Agg to everything that moves and especially pains vampires through R\u00f6tschreck.<br /> <br /> You see the results in the many, many, many discussions about how using fire is OP, fire powers are OP, fire this and that, despite fire IRL being at the same time everything the game says, yet most definitely not a common weapon in real conflicts.<br /> <br /> Why fire is a big deal?<br /> <br /> 1. It bites deep, no way around it. Any rule that tries to &quot;balance&quot; things by making fire cause less damage is missing the memo of why firefighters are firefighters, not just &quot;rescuers&quot;.<br /> <br /> 2. Fire is almost uncontrollable by nature. Yes, the Pyro is a fun unit in Team Fortress 2. IRL no one uses flamethrowers as weapons because it's stupid: they require extreme care and attention, are horrible for fine control and &quot;aim&quot;, and the user is basically stuck in place without being able to react to an actual combat as they are instead focused on applying fire without dying in the process, preferably also not destroying everything else than their target.<br /> <br /> 3. Fire isn't subtle. You don't cover up a fire, at most you cover up with fire. It causes a mess and leaves a mess. You can certainly destroy evidence with fire, but it's an admission that you destroyed evidence in the first place. Trying to quietly off vampires with fire is like putting silencers on frag grenades.<br /> <br /> 4. Fire is a known, constant danger. Our houses and apartments are built with fire safety protocols almost whenever you live in the world. Everything that uses fire is also full of warnings and safety instructions. And fire still causes accidents all the time, everywhere.<br /> <br /> 5. With all that, fire is easy to produce. You see discussions about how a given fire power is broken, when the PC could simply dump gas on something and put it on fire for same or worse results.<br /> <br /> 6. IRL fire-based weapons and ammo simply aren't what the hype says because they either:<br /> - Don't put anything on fire. Most phosphorus bullets and similar are just tracers without any remaining fire on them when hit target (and frequently less lethal because of deformation)<br /> - Work, and get all the same problems in the process. Dragon Breath ammo is an excellent example of a casing that robs your gun from all the benefits of being a gun and make it work as a glorified flamethrower. It has good technical reasons to exist, but they put it back squarely in the &quot;sensitive tool&quot; category of regular flamethrowers.<br /> - serve specific tactical purposes instead of being general use weapons, usually when the lack of control is the goal. Molotovs aren't for targeted destruction. They're for starting fires.<br /> - only exist for show. A lot of very cool weapons are actually bad at combat, but good at demonstration.<br /> <br /> Characters that use fire as weapons in media usually subtly cancel everything out by skill or fiat. What makes them cool is that they use fire, but what makes their use of fire cool is that they avoid all the problems. Is that the best approach for VtM?<br /> <br /> PROPOSAL<br /> <br /> - Control Fire Action (Wits + Resolve)<br /> Whenever you want to use, feed, start or put down fire, this is a Control action, no matter how big or small, no matter your purpose. It doesn't always require a roll, but it is always its own thing with its own time.<br /> - Crucially, that means you can't make attack or defensive rolls with fire, period. You can use it in combat, but only as a Control Fire Action. In V5 that means you don't participate in the regular combat contested roll, you forfeit.<br /> - Mechanically it's a decision tree where you start small and slow, then &quot;buy&quot; your outcome out of this paying with the risk of a fizzle or a backfire.<br /> <br /> Step 1: state your goal. Lighting a cigarette is a goal, putting the building on fire is a goal. Throwing a match in a tank of gasoline is not a goal, the goal is if you want it to catch fire or fizzle.<br /> Step 2: choose between Realistic and Dramatic fire.<br /> - Realistic is slow and safe, and has to be. Any resistance, hindrance or failure at planning means you back down. If everything is ok, no roll, you just do it.<br /> - Dramatic is risky, but is faster and don't back down. Roll Fire Control, if you fail, the fire gets out of control and you're probably the first/next to get a taste of it.<br /> - You can add Tactical rolls to plan ahead how the fire will spread, or Cinematic uses where the fire glows bright, but most of the heat is lost.<br /> <br /> Open to discussion, but that's the gist of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-19T04:14:45-07:00","id":1560751,"title":"","content":"In time I will consolidate everything in the first post and add decent formatting.<br /> <br /> Decapitation and Metaphysics<br /> <br /> The problem: sometimes the game undersells how and why some aspects are important because it just flows with intuition without question if it should.<br /> <br /> Vampires get specifically rules for decapitation and it is an instant death sentence. All well and good, nothing weird, except for three things:<br /> <br /> 1. Why decapitation was singled out as the insta-kill method if there are other forms of non survivable trauma? Why not heart removal, crushed head or torso, struck by lightning, or a lot of other extremes?<br /> <br /> 2. Given that the fact is at first self-explanatory, the game really need decapitation mechanics?<br /> <br /> 3. If vampires are supernatural undead beings, is &quot;intuition&quot; really the correct approach here? It seems easy to run with gut feeling, but should we?<br /> <br /> Our greatest problem is less the game having rules or if they're good, and more that it doesn't usually dedicate nearly enough space to explain why they are there and why they matter.<br /> <br /> Why decapitation is relevant?<br /> <br /> 1. It's not for the intuitive reason, and this is an important info that shouldn't be glossed over. Vampires are animated corpses. Their hearts and brains don't work. A vampire's mind is not housed in their head. Observe that only beheading is dangerous to vampires no other trauma to the head is.<br /> <br /> 2. This applies to all their organs. Everything a vampire does is because magic, not any physical property of the biological substrate. This substrate is dead and could just as well be rocks and stuffing. In fact, it's perfectly possible that a vampire would be mostly unaffected by brain removal if it's not a beheading.<br /> <br /> 3. Removing the head instead has old history as a ceremonial action, because the head is a symbol of identity. It is where your face is, and what matters metaphysically isn't your brain, but eyes, mouth, expression. After all, for most of history it was clear the house of thought and emotion was your stomach.<br /> <br /> 4. Beheadings aren't just effective executions, they are ceremonial. Exposing the head of the deceased is a powerful symbolic act be it in a stake as a warning or in a glass chamber as an object of veneration. Beheadings are ritualized and full of rules even when just a secular execution. The Guillotine was and is divisive as an execution instrument because of how it impacts the significance of the act.<br /> <br /> 5. Crucially, those many aspects converged in the rituals to explain or deal with the unquiet dead. What causes someone to come back from the grave? A corpse to refuse rest? The ghost to linger or suffer or find peace? How do you correct the problem? While the answers are not universal or anything, they frequently include the state of the head.<br /> <br /> 6. In particular at the time VtM was written we have two important influences: the rituals of the Vampyr (which is not a vampire, but the influence is kinda obvious) and Revenant in Europe in recent centuries, where it was usually recommended to behead the body, stuff the mouth with holy offerings and place it carefully back to give rest to the deceased. And Highlander because it was seriously big on VtM creators at the time. The whole Diablerie is pure Highlander influence.<br /> <br /> Only after this we can examine if the rules are needed or good. And that Highlander influence left the questionable default of beheading as a combat action rather than ritual performance.<br /> <br /> PROPOSAL<br /> <br /> - Enduring Corpse (new baseline)<br /> Ditch the part of the rules that say you meet Final Death if you keep taking damage after going into Torpor. Full Agg is the limit of harm a vampire suffers. The real extension of how this body is now a supernatural abomination rather than a normal corpse is visible at this point: sometimes it resists hacking as if made of unbreakable metal, sometimes it repairs when no one is looking, sometimes it's just that no matter what punishment you dish, enough of its integrity always remains.<br /> Under this rule killing a vampire for good isn't necessarily hard, but is a deliberate ritual and not something that just happens. You must find and chose an actual execution that works, and here beheading becomes important.<br /> - Final Death (Intelligence + Occult, Dif 1 + Bane Severity)<br /> Not even the Banes of vampirism will just put one to rest eternally by themselves. You have to use a proper execution like beheading, stake or burning, but you also have to follow procedure. Sometimes setting for torpor and sealing is the only option against the most powerful monsters.<br /> RATIONALE: this makes the distinction of what things actually kill vampires more relevant, and add rules for actual execution scenes where the destruction isn't just the result of combat, but a deliberate dramatic action. It also drives home why a given method is a &quot;threat to vampires&quot; rather than just a flavor of violence.<br /> <br /> - Cursed Integrity (optional)<br /> The ST can always decide the vampire suffers the normal constraints of having an organ missing, and if you don't want a mauled corpse as a functional PC you can just keep them alive but torpid. But whenever you want, this rule is simple: a vampire only depends on its body for physical actions in the strictest sense. Since no organ has actual biological function, no function requires an organ. When the vampire lacks a sense like vision, for exemple, it's an effect of their particular metaphysics and not a lack of functional eyes, since no vampire has functional eyes anyway. The character suffers no ill effects of the state of the body, even if that means seeing, hearing and sniffing things without a working head. Rules work as normal, but description of their physical integrity has no bearing on anything.<br /> RATIONALE: that helps remove the sensation that something should hinder a vampire because it should hinder a mortal. It's not a hard rule, but a remainder that those monsters don't work through biology whatsoever.<br /> <br /> - The Catalog of Destruction (optional)<br /> The ST can and should mess around with what is needed for a vampire to truly die. It can change by chronicle, or within chronicle depend on area, era or individual vampire. Not always a beheading or immolation will do the trick even if they remain general Banes, sometimes they may even make things worse.<br /> At the same time, previous Final Deaths can establish reliable methods, and maybe replicating an entry in the chronicle's Catalog do bypass the need for a roll.<br /> RATIONALE: the Catalog is where the ST make the decision on what to keep as certain Final Death if they feel the need. Instead of ambiguous rules, you curate your own authoritative guide on vampire killing.<br /> <br /> What about beheading in combat?<br /> <br /> Do as thy will, it cease being the important rule, just that. If your game needs a beheading rule, keep it. If not, drop it. Beheading instantly fill anyone with Agg +1 dmg, with results covered by the rest of the system, and your Catalog is the authority on if it is Final Death or not.<br /> <br /> Removing the rule doesn't remove the descriptive existence of beheadings just as you don't need specific rules for every conceivable injury.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-19T11:19:41-07:00","id":1560762,"title":"","content":"I think that's skipping the folklore inspiration a bit. Stakes through the heart (I know, that's next) and decapitations are both parts of European folklore around how to deal with vampires. While details are wildly variable, it was a cultural belief that exhuming a suspected vampire from the grave and decapitating it would either remove the ability for the vampire to rise out of the grave, or permanently destroy it, or whatever else in-between; again it's folk myth. Whatever the details, what persisted into the pop-culture vampires that VtM draws from is that they can't operate properly if decapitated and VtM chose to do the &quot;this kills them&quot; route all the way back in 1e. Calling it intuition and how that doesn't work feels like it's missing the point. Decapitation is a mythological &quot;weakness&quot; of vampires like sunlight. There's no rational scientific reasoning behind it, it works when other things that would normally kill a human but don't because vampires are magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-19T11:27:54-07:00","id":1560764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560762#post1560762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that's skipping the folklore inspiration a bit. Stakes through the heart (I know, that's next) and decapitations are both parts of European folklore around how to deal with vampires. While details are wildly variable, it was a cultural belief that exhuming a suspected vampire from the grave and decapitating it would either remove the ability for the vampire to rise out of the grave, or permanently destroy it, or whatever else in-between; again it's folk myth. Whatever the details, what persisted into the pop-culture vampires that VtM draws from is that they can't operate properly if decapitated and VtM chose to do the &quot;this kills them&quot; route all the way back in 1e. Calling it intuition and how that doesn't work feels like it's missing the point. Decapitation is a mythological &quot;weakness&quot; of vampires like sunlight. There's no rational scientific reasoning behind it, it works when other things that would normally kill a human but don't because vampires are magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's what I tried to convey in point 5. I know the reasoning exists, it's just that the texts doesn't convey it well.<br /> <br /> Overall my complaint isn't &quot;the game shouldn't have this rule&quot;, it's &quot;the game should have explained better&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-19T11:34:22-07:00","id":1560765,"title":"","content":"I guess, but your post largely ends with removing the rule or rendering it irrelevant; not just explaining it in a better fashion with some adjustments to fit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-20T06:49:19-07:00","id":1560782,"title":"","content":"I think the ceremonial execution actually covers it better than a combat beheading rule. The last one throws away all the actual scenes of corpse trials and exhumations to focus on Highlander.<br /> <br /> The adjustment is to get it out of combat mechanics as a ninja action to bring to a rule that will make beheading the forefront of the scene. The combat mechanic is still there for Highlander scenes.<br /> <br /> Wooden Stakes and Combat<br /> <br /> The problem: the game says it is more horror than action. It says narrative and drama take precedence over combat. It says combat rules are there to reinforce desperation.<br /> <br /> It delivers a cinematic rule based on action stories that use goth aesthetics for impossible testosterone-ladden violence rather than an actual horror-based rule.<br /> <br /> 1. Stakes are crude pieces of wood by design. They are not weapons and don't work as such. They're not adequately sharpened or hard or anything to work as such. They have no aerodynamics, they are bulky, they're intending and soft. And have to go through a reinforced ribcage to reach a heart.<br /> <br /> 2. The traditional depiction of a Hunter driving one through a vampire chest isn't about aiming a bizarre stake with a medieval-stylized Tommy Gun in horror literature. It's a careful work, attentive to the intervention of servants or the sudden awaken of the monster, while a strong man position the stake with care and hit it with a fucking maul. Repeatedly. Because you're trying to break through bone plating.<br /> <br /> 3. Combat delivery IRL would result on success with splinters at best, while the main stake bounces, scratches or breakers because that's what a actual stake does when its treated as a bullet or dagger rather than a wooden stake.<br /> <br /> This is a similar problem to the beheadings, except way worse because rules focused since old times into making this even easier to pull in combat as it is a common trope. It recognizes the importance of the trope, but acknowledge only the Blade delivery.<br /> <br /> PROPOSAL: the rules above cover a lot, and the combat stake is still an option for cinematic hunters. That's for games that want to lean less on combat for any reason and cash in the tradicional staking.<br /> <br /> - Mystically Inconspicuous (Strength + Medicine)<br /> Stakes are not weapons, and few people have any reason to think otherwise. Even those few who knows about vampires are more likely to think you want to go camping or build a fence than try to kill someone with this thing.<br /> This is not a supernatural effect by itself, you're not going to be ignored as if your stake is Obfuscated, except against torpid vampires.<br /> In this rule, wooden stakes work as charms against the vampire itself:<br /> - Carrying the stake gives a -1 dice penalty to the vampire on rolls to notice invasion, or +1 dif if applicable.<br /> - Handling the stake gives a -2 dice penalty to those rolls if the vampire is still sleeping. You'll give no doubt of your intentions if seen, but have a slightly better chance at not being seen.<br /> - Hammering the stake down takes a Strength + Medicine roll, dif 2 or more. Success paralyzes de Vampire immediately for a round, while extra successes accumulate for the more permanent effect. You must achieve Bane Severity in extra successes to finished the job.<br /> - Failure means the stake is dislodged enough for the injured and pissed-off vampire to revide.<br /> RATIONALE: this is not an easy endeavor, but a stake is rendered indeed the simplest way to paralyze a vampire without engaging in combat at all, which should be priority. It isn't fast and requires access to the vampire, but reliably getting 2 successes is enough to remove the undead at least from combat as long as you keep pressure on heart, not stomach.<br /> <br /> Beheading in combat at least is a thing. Stake in combat, only if you really want the cinematic flavor. For horror or realism it's not a good option .<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-20T15:18:47-07:00","id":1560795,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560762#post1560762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that's skipping the folklore inspiration a bit. Stakes through the heart (I know, that's next) and decapitations are both parts of European folklore around how to deal with vampires. While details are wildly variable, it was a cultural belief that exhuming a suspected vampire from the grave and decapitating it would either remove the ability for the vampire to rise out of the grave, or permanently destroy it, or whatever else in-between; again it's folk myth. Whatever the details, what persisted into the pop-culture vampires that VtM draws from is that they can't operate properly if decapitated and VtM chose to do the &quot;this kills them&quot; route all the way back in 1e. Calling it intuition and how that doesn't work feels like it's missing the point. Decapitation is a mythological &quot;weakness&quot; of vampires like sunlight. There's no rational scientific reasoning behind it, it works when other things that would normally kill a human but don't because vampires are magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is... Is staking just derived from literally nailing the poor corpse to it's coffin so it can't get up?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28924-tjalei","name":"Tjalei","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-20T15:25:35-07:00","id":1560796,"title":"","content":"We're never going to have an objective accounting of how folklore developed. It's rare that you can reliably trace the development of these concepts. Did stakes start as some idea that certain woods could impede the flow of blood in an animate corpse and then get conflated with nailing corpses into their coffins, or was it an expansion of the concept of nailing corpses down? We'll never really know. We don't have the records for anthropologists to tease these things apart. The best we can do is look at more recent pop-culture iterations on folklore. We largely know what Bram Stoker was pulling from - and it wasn't some attempt to create an &quot;authentic&quot; vampire - in different parts of creating <i>Dracula, </i>because we have is notes from research for the novel. So we can say, &quot;he knew about this story, and that legend, and this myth,&quot; and generally have a solid idea of why he picked what he did. We don't have that knowledge for older stories.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-20T18:43:44-07:00","id":1560800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tjalei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560795#post1560795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Is... Is staking just derived from literally nailing the poor corpse to it's coffin so it can't get up?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd actually &quot;disagree&quot; with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> here, but in another aspect of the account:<br /> <br /> We can be sure it isn't just about pinning the corpse to the coffin because however it was at first, it evolved.<br /> <br /> People developed new explanations and reasonings, new methods. The stake we depict today is probably way different from the original. It certainly isn't to nail to the coffin as it hardly reaches the heart.<br /> <br /> What of previous ones? They could have been symbols of gods, traditions around wood, replications of other ceremonies. The Vampyr is a corpse that haras their living family and sometimes get into bed with their spouse, the stake could come from an ancient divorce ritual. Who knows?<br /> <br /> And it can have been all of those at some point, especially if it isn't a linear evolution, but the consolidation of originally distinct beliefs.<br /> <br /> Either way rituals could vary a lot, but most of the time they involved decapitation or staking the corpse. Keeping in mind those were specifically for cases where a corpse was deemed suspect of coming back, which usually meant exhumating and finding out the body was still well preserved, which could happen for a number of factors but was not a rule.<br /> <br /> If actual church authorities were involved, you would hardly even get to the exhumation part. A few ceremonial blessings and a bunch of blessed items would do the trick of calming either the ghost or the demons pretending to be them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-20T22:32:15-07:00","id":1560804,"title":"","content":"Cold and Extreme Threats<br /> <br /> Problem: everything. Not a single line there is good. The idea is and a lot, but the execution has all possible errors, including scientific ones, which is always a wonder in a game where you can magic away your problems.<br /> <br /> 1. The rule kicks in at -30\u2070 (-22 in Freedom Units). You start to run against freezing that was ignored before.<br /> 2. Despite described as an insidious danger, it's a temperature only seen in nature in very few times and locations, and even in industrial settings only when you leave the realm of &quot;efficient refrigeration&quot; for &quot;flash-freeze or more radical stuff&quot;.<br /> 3. The torpor is kind of lame here. The vamp is already immobilized. How cooler is a frozen undead that still projects thoughts to nearby targets than just &quot;you have 1 hour to telepath your friends, then torpor&quot;<br /> 4. It's overall bad because it attacks the trope of the arctic vampire without offering anything in exchange, it seems to simply state &quot;if a mortal can't survive there, you can't do much better&quot;.<br /> 5. I'm unaware of any interesting story about a vampire being frozen to harmlessness.<br /> 6. The last paragraph on water is a barrage of stupidity. At -30 water is frozen. Water below the ice cap of a river or something is liquid precisely because it's not at -30, it's around 0.<br /> 7. And vampires don't sink in cold water more than warm water, which depends because the whole argument is bullshit. The human body has lots of low density tissue and averages close to water density. Float or sink without air depends on person. And vampires have lungs, they just don't need the oxygen, but taking a breath to float is kinda an option.<br /> <br /> PROPOSAL:<br /> <br /> - Cold penalties, no roll<br /> Below 0\u2070C - cosmetic changes, -2 dice to pass for mortal as your skin won't thaw ice.<br /> Below -20\u2070C - undeath fights brittleness, but movement becomes visibly stiff (no penalty in rolls, but can't be hidden). Suffer 1 Superficial Health dmg when reaching it, but is not a recurring damage. Instead, cycling thaw/freeze is worse than stable freeze.<br /> Below -40\u2070C - Brittleness kicks. Add +1 to any damage suffered. -2 to physical actions, though you can accept 1 Superficial Health dmg to forego the penalty.<br /> Below -60\u2070C - if the Curse's magic fails, the body is too brittle for any action without taking damage, the vampire is living, but immobile.<br /> - There's no saying when the magic will be overwhelmed, below -60\u2070 and without special powers nothing the vampire can do about it. It may also forego the Night Waking Rouse Check and the Daysleep, resulting in a body paralyzed without rest, forever.<br /> RATIONALE: greater range of activity and I hope better scientific grounding while using the magic of undeath to simplify stuff. A real body won't get full solid 'till close to -70 because it isn't just water, but will get brittle in a nonlinear progression. Particularly, the ST is free to hold things together indefinitely if they want.<br /> <br /> - Cold References (that was greatly missing)<br /> 2-4\u2070 - Pelagic temperature. Deep waters don't get colder than that. It's possible at that pressure to be liquid way below 0\u2070, but it's not what we see in natural water bodies.<br /> 0\u2070 - freezing water. It may be either liquid or solid if pure, it's transitioning.<br /> -10\u2070 - most temperate coast will hardly get colder than that. Commercial refrigerators. Ice film can form on surfaces.<br /> -20\u2070 - this is where tourists seek extreme cold, the litmus for comfortable life even with house heating. Some lab equipment and industrial freezers that need extra safety but are still freezers work at -18\u2070. Rubber and plastic lose flexibility, even metal is noticeably brittle and most fuel start to thicken or gel.<br /> -30\u2070 - the coldest inhabitable environments for most people. Deep freeze warehouses and cryogenics test chambers work here. It's the limit of cold storage before changing the very meaning of cold storage.<br /> -40\u2070 - in inhabited areas this is possible, but catastrophic. This is a pharmaceutical ultras freezer, used for regular cryogenic facilities and some vaccines. The aerospace industry has chambers that cold to search the worse conditions they face, and some materials labs to study changes in matter.<br /> -50\u2070 - natural cold like that isn't just rare, demands deliberate search for real. Some cooling chambers fed by liquid nitrogen can reach it. Otherwise, facilities this cold are for research purposes, only.<br /> -70\u2070 - the coldest regions on Earth, period. The most specialized cryogenic facilities work at this. Others are labs working at space conditions or on absolute material failure tests. The most resistant oil is starting to freeze while some metals start becoming superconductors.<br /> <br /> Other things happen, as the cold changes the environment in unexpected ways, like air clearing, colors changing, the physical properties of snow and ice changing.<br /> <br /> For water, do nothing.<br /> <br /> Water in dangerous to mortals between 0\u2070 and -10\u2070 because it increases heat loss tremendously. But by the time a vampire cares, it will most probably be solid already.<br /> <br /> What's more interesting though, and the game missed: if we have rules for cryogenic facilities, why not other extreme environment a character can face in a modern city?<br /> <br /> - furnaces<br /> - molten metal/glass<br /> - chemical vats<br /> - wind tunnels<br /> - hyper centrifuges<br /> <br /> Seeing how vampires survive or even use such spaces as havens would be awesome.<br /> <br /> By the way, last note:<br /> <br /> Ignore p. 223 on Final Death reached by deep sea pressure pulping the body. This is not a thing.<br /> <br /> We're used to uneven pressure. Hydrostatic pressure won't do that. A theoretical diver that went all the way down to the bottom of the Mariana Trench would be physically intact.<br /> <br /> Dead, sure, but because of lack of oxygen (not enough lung strength to keep breathing) and some chemical changes in body caused by pressure (a lot o Nitrogen buildup). Not injury, the body would be fine.<br /> <br /> It also contradicts lore, as a great number of vampires exist all or part of the time at great, even abyssal Dept's.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-21T03:58:45-07:00","id":1560811,"title":"","content":"Isn't the main issue with pressure mostly how fast you go down or up? And that is what causes pressure damage? Though I'd imagine at some point there'd be enough pressure to kill you, but that's probably a very high limit. I'd have to ask an expert to figure out the answer though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13674-amalasuintha","name":"Amalasuintha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13674_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-21T04:27:41-07:00","id":1560812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amalasuintha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560811#post1560811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't the main issue with pressure mostly how fast you go down or up? And that is what causes pressure damage? Though I'd imagine at some point there'd be enough pressure to kill you, but that's probably a very high limit. I'd have to ask an expert to figure out the answer though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not really an expert, but diving sickness (&quot;the bends&quot;) and rapid (de)compression are different things. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> pointed out, the bends are due to nitrogen accumulation which vampires wouldn't care about much.<br /> <br /> A sudden pressure differential on the other hand causes physical damage. As the pressures try to equalize, they can produce enough force to tear a body apart. You could look up the Byford Dolphin incident for an example, but it's nightmare fuel you may want to skip. Anyways, it's only a problem for really fast changes in pressure, ie an explosion or implosion.<br /> <br /> Even if the vampire would ascend or descend with full Celerity, they would never be fast enough to cause this to happen as the pressure change happens gradually.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-21T04:40:41-07:00","id":1560813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amalasuintha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560811#post1560811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't the main issue with pressure mostly how fast you go down or up? And that is what causes pressure damage? Though I'd imagine at some point there'd be enough pressure to kill you, but that's probably a very high limit. I'd have to ask an expert to figure out the answer though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I studied it some time ago, so no sources. Then yes, if you have an expert that would be a good call, better yet if they can point a good free source.<br /> <br /> But long story short two things change the effects of pressure: uniformity and how much the fluid around you compresses under pressure.<br /> <br /> Most of our experience with pressure under air is uneven and dealing with a medium that compresses a lot. That leads to squishy results as the fluid in question moves violently to weak spots.<br /> <br /> Hydrostatic pressure is the opposite. Very hard to concentrate, tending to get uniformly around you, and water is stupidly resistant to compression. A drop from the surface and one from the Mariana Trenches have almost the same density.<br /> <br /> All in all the result is no damage. Other things do happen. Without magicking physics away, a vampire in deep water would be pinned by the weight of the water column in a way air doesn't do. Think being unable to raise even if less dense because the pressure is literally forcing you down.<br /> <br /> The problem with speed is chemical. Pressure changes reactivity and solubility of a lot of things that we have in the body. In this case the main culprit is your blood's Nitrogen. As you compress it will suffer some changes, if you then decompress too fast it will form bubbles in your veins.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-21T13:20:44-07:00","id":1560823,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560812#post1560812\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not really an expert, but diving sickness (&quot;the bends&quot;) and rapid (de)compression are different things. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> pointed out, the bends are due to nitrogen accumulation which vampires wouldn't care about much.<br /> <br /> A sudden pressure differential on the other hand causes physical damage. As the pressures try to equalize, they can produce enough force to tear a body apart. You could look up the Byford Dolphin incident for an example, but it's nightmare fuel you may want to skip. Anyways, it's only a problem for really fast changes in pressure, ie an explosion or implosion.<br /> <br /> Even if the vampire would ascend or descend with full Celerity, they would never be fast enough to cause this to happen as the pressure change happens gradually.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given I've been eating sweet things tonight, I think I'll skip the nightmare fuel this time. o.o<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560813#post1560813\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I studied it some time ago, so no sources. Then yes, if you have an expert that would be a good call, better yet if they can point a good free source.<br /> <br /> But long story short two things change the effects of pressure: uniformity and how much the fluid around you compresses under pressure.<br /> <br /> Most of our experience with pressure under air is uneven and dealing with a medium that compresses a lot. That leads to squishy results as the fluid in question moves violently to weak spots.<br /> <br /> Hydrostatic pressure is the opposite. Very hard to concentrate, tending to get uniformly around you, and water is stupidly resistant to compression. A drop from the surface and one from the Mariana Trenches have almost the same density.<br /> <br /> All in all the result is no damage. Other things do happen. Without magicking physics away, a vampire in deep water would be pinned by the weight of the water column in a way air doesn't do. Think being unable to raise even if less dense because the pressure is literally forcing you down.<br /> <br /> The problem with speed is chemical. Pressure changes reactivity and solubility of a lot of things that we have in the body. In this case the main culprit is your blood's Nitrogen. As you compress it will suffer some changes, if you then decompress too fast it will form bubbles in your veins.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah I understand it now. Thank you for your explanations. I know very little about pressure stuff beyond it being dangerous depending on the situation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13674-amalasuintha","name":"Amalasuintha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13674_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-25T17:54:46-07:00","id":1561011,"title":"","content":"Started to edit the main, now I want to discuss rules for other extreme conditions related to the modern industrial world.<br /> <br /> Those are not places we usually consider as anything beyond cool hazards, because the human body can't survive them. But inhuman corpses may be able to withstand at least enough to explore or use many of them further than humans, and even use some as safe if inconvenient havens, where hunters can't hope to get into.<br /> <br /> I'm not making rules now, I want to discuss ideas about what to do with them. I'll also do research later.<br /> <br /> - Furnaces:<br /> <br /> Of course a vampire can't resist the fire of such places. But industrial furnaces include whole machines and areas of intense <i>heat </i>and heat is not the same as fire.<br /> <br /> I think vampires should be able to ignore heat at least until water boils, and probably higher. Not indefinitely, of course.<br /> <br /> The dangers are four: boiling, melting, cooking and burning. Each happens to a human body at a different temperature, cause different injuries and may interact with the supernatural body of a vampire in different ways.<br /> <br /> Which means the Curse can be used to wave the physics and damage for a certain degree. For example, we may decide Vampire Blood within the body only boils at 150\u2070C, or never and the boiling itself is never a source of harm.<br /> <br /> It can also be used to make some serious problems into mere penalties. This is useful as a way to open up story options: if exploring an area of intense heat creates obstacles without killing, your PCs can try them at a cost.<br /> <br /> - Molten rock/glass:<br /> <br /> At lower ends, lava is less destructive than we usually think. First it's so dense it doesn't really feel liquid, second its heat doesn't transfer that well. Finally, it actually cools down at a reasonably fast rate.<br /> <br /> In lab experiments recreating lava, pieces of meat or chicken submerged in lava burn in the outside but remain uncooked inside: the immediate rock solidifies and insulate from the rest of the flow. A vampire should be able to survive, despite entombed in rock and with some damage.<br /> <br /> Glass would pose a similar situation, but more common and easier to break free.<br /> <br /> - Chemical vats:<br /> <br /> Those depend on each kind of chemical. Some are corrosive and would eat at vampire flesh, or react producing heat and fire. But others are toxic to humans for thoroughly distinct reasons that an undead body doesn't care.<br /> <br /> Another option for exploring those is to draw on fluids dynamics to see how different vats change movement: is it possible to swim, or is the vampire consigned to either the bottom or the surface? Is it so viscous you get totally stuck, or is it surprisingly easier than water? What about non-newtonian fluids?<br /> <br /> - Centrifuges:<br /> <br /> Here the problem is definitely something that can smash your body to a pulp, the G force. It partially fights gravity and each level makes movement harder and harder.<br /> <br /> Big enough centrifuges for a human body are uncommon, but not unheard of: some materials are processed in bulk in big ones, and some are built for human use in gravity tests. A fictional setting with secret laboratories has probably a number of bigger, weirder ones, or other machinery that work like one.<br /> <br /> - Wind tunnels:","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 06-25-2026, 09:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T06:55:34-07:00","id":1561763,"title":"","content":"I'm working on each idea from before to expand options, and started to look at the abomination that are rules for incendiary weapons in TTRPGs in general.<br /> <br /> My goal is to write a comprehensive rule that empowers the ST to set them themselves, but I have first to take out of my chest the absurdity found in V5 core (and other editions aren't innocent).<br /> <br /> Dragon Breath Ammo: according to V5 the round stick to the target, burning.<br /> <br /> - Dragon Breath is a pyrotechnics round. It shoots bright sparks, a lot of them. It isn't too strong, won't go really far and, while it can burn things, it's not that much.<br /> - It's also incredibly stupid and forbidden on most places for good reason. It's worse than regular fireworks, considerably less safe and is bad to the shotgun, requiring careful deep cleaning every time and still guaranteeing it'll break way faster.<br /> <br /> Raufoss: .50 rifle shell, ignores body armor and explodes in fire inside the body.<br /> <br /> - Raufoss is anti-material. That means it's for use against vehicles. It won't shoot from any rifle and it won't explode inside someone, it is specifically made to only explode after 5 seconds, guaranteeing it'll pass through any unfortunate human body and hit the actual vehicle.<br /> <br /> Handflammpatrone/HAFLA: shoot up to 80 meters, it will explode in fire and coat the target, requiring submersion + scrub to remove. It's relatively easy to reproduce.<br /> <br /> - This is an Area Denial weapon from the Cold War, discontinued because of failure rate.<br /> - Production requires highly specialized facility and specialized workers, the kind you find in a high-level military weapons factory.<br /> - Straight in front of you it shoots up to 8m. Only shooting upwards it can reach 70-90m (exploding by timer if didn't hit anything).<br /> - the fire covers aprox. an 8m diameter area. It won't coat your target, it will cost everything.<br /> - burns for 2minutes, not particularly hard to remove, though it may be hard to stop because it's white phosphorus.<br /> <br /> WWII Flamethrower: coats target in sticky fuel that needs water + scrub to remove, burns for a long time, set your surroundings ablaze.<br /> <br /> - coats everything in a cone with not really sticky or strong fuel, the main goal is to set an area ablaze, a target will probably survive. It's also hard as hell to control, so &quot;target&quot; is kind of an exaggeration.<br /> <br /> Molotov Cocktail: &quot;a real Molotov is made with petrol jelly and a sparkle, not gasoline and rag&quot;. Hit ground, covers the target in not so sticky fuel, fire is stable, removing clothes advisable, but is done in a single action along with stopping the fire in the body.<br /> <br /> - Definitely a real Molotov is a glass bottle with gasoline and a rag. Some people try petrol jelly to make it slightly more dangerous, and/or add a proper ignition to save fuel and increase effective spread.<br /> - Area denial. It's very hard to hit people with this in purpose. Main targets are vehicles, walls or just the floor to make an area of fire.<br /> - if it does not someone, isn't lethal but takes quite some time to put down the fire and get rid of your clothes, as I guess anyone who uses clothes know.<br /> <br /> On all the sticky fuels needing scrubbing: half or more or the descriptions ignore the actual weapons and go for Napalm. You don't use Napalm as fuel for any of those weapons, it's a disaster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T09:31:12-07:00","id":1562146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1560762#post1560762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that's skipping the folklore inspiration a bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm revising this part, and with this took the time to make a proper research into the matter.<br /> <br /> What I can say today is that we're looking to at least two very distinct lines of development for this trope, where the distinction is in the very concept of what the revenant is.<br /> <br /> Folklore left remarkably little record about the beliefs behind such practices. Many others we have a lot of writings about how the monsters and phenomena are understood, their meaning, etc. For raising corpses it's almost nothing but the practices themselves.<br /> <br /> Current anthropologic theories for this, based in the matter-of-fact style of descriptions and the few theories on the mechanism behind it, is that corpses were seen indeed as mechanical objects rather than &quot;rekindled&quot; in any way.<br /> <br /> Some theories are eminently mechanical, like a common idea that gases building up could be them cause for reanimation, while others were supernatural, with it becoming a vessel for a spirit, possibly but not necessarily that of the deceased. But they all agree on key points: the body itself is just an unintelligent mechanism, a literal vehicle at best. Cursed dead people are the purview of ghosts and possessions.<br /> <br /> The common line this <i>appears</i> to draw among the distinct practices is that they stay purely mechanical: stuff the mouth so the body can't bite or gnaw, pierce the heart so it can't pump fluids, and remove the head and place it in the body's legs so getting up becomes impossible. This reading is enforced by the belief that risk is directly tied to the state of decomposition, not as much with preserved corpses seen as unnatural, but any fresh corpse being a greater risk as decomposition removes the problem. This isn't very different in Asia, with the original Chinese &quot;vampires&quot; being just stiff corpses that monks would animate to ease their travel to the body's village for burial.<br /> <br /> Vampires proper appear in literature later, first what we can call &quot;proto-vampires&quot; from 1800 onward, then something closer with Polidori's The Vampyre. And they start <b><i>cut completely</i></b> from this folklore.<br /> <br /> Both proto-vampires and Lord Ruthven are not particularly resilient. Geraldine (1800), Oneiza (1801) and Darley (1817) aren't described as immortals, with Oneiza dying from common violence. Ruthven is similarly killed by bandits, and he brings the notion of immortality coming back to life after a year rather than resisting it in the first place.<br /> <br /> Our first example of the immortal vampire as we know is Varney, but he's immune to all the methods. Varney dies in the end by jumping into an active volcano, the old equivalent of the character being tied to a rocket to the Sun.<br /> <br /> Real folkloric methods would come back only with Carmilla, but they're immediately beyond their original scope: her destruction requires a lengthy ritual with many blessings and prays. Several methods aren't applied in succession, with a blessed stake through her heart, then she is beheaded with a blessed blade, and then her body is burned. It's severely implied that anything less she would still n\u00e9 able to recover from.<br /> <br /> Dracula keeps a similar approach for Lucy, and Van Helsing's studies imply she's not the only one, but the Count himself requires something different, and although we see the last of him with a Bowie Knife in the chest, it's ambiguous in the book, after all the struggle to kill him, which parts contributed or not.<br /> <br /> Even setting aside my peeve with decapitation as a combat occurrence, VtM sits in a weird space in the tradition. Old stories would accept simple beheading, but they describe an entity very different from modern Vampires and, as it seems, more mechanical. New ones would not engage with beheading unless if it needed far more effort than just doing it.<br /> <br /> Mostly VtM's take seems to still come from Highlander rather than anything vampire.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T16:35:29-07:00","id":1562164,"title":"","content":"One other element which might effect the decapitation orientation of vampire other than Highlander (but I agree that was def part of it) is the Kennedy assassinations impact in popular culture.<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t know if this is actually backed up by the scholarship or is instead an urban legend or just-so story, but I recall hearing the argument somewhere that prior to 11/22/63, the typical means of carrying out or depicting a killing was to target the torso, with the heart being the symbolic seat of life (fitting with the above discussed heart-piercing in vampire slayings). The pivot towards a focus on the head, at least according to this argument, is partly due to the popularization of psychology and increased perception of the brain as the seat of the self and a memetic replication of one of the most publicized killings in modern history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T20:59:52-07:00","id":1562168,"title":"","content":"Considering how long decapitations were a means of execution in history, I highly doubt there was some shift in the 1960s. France performed its last execution by guillotine in 1977.. that's a pretty long history of cutting heads off.<br /> <br /> In combat, the logic of targeting the center mass has always been very simple: it's the biggest target and it's full of vital organs. Landing solid hits to the head is much harder compared to attacks to the torso. In lots of existing manuals of sword fighting in the age of plate armor, one of the default targets was to thrust to the underside of the shoulder joint (which has to be loose for flexibility) to disable you're opponent's arm and then you pull a dagger to finish them off. Helmets were plenty strong to keep the head safe from a lot of blows and those things were designed to that thrusts or arrows to the necessary sight and breathing holes were flukes rather than realistic targets. Guns didn't change the calculus. Shooting for center mass instead of the head has been the default for centuries and still is.<br /> <br /> The idea this has anything to do with changing concepts of a symbolic nature to the heart and the brain doesn't really line up with how fights work, or even with when medicine and anatomy started to sort out which organs did what.<br /> <br /> ---------<br /> <br /> My only issue with the whole &quot;Highlander&quot; thing is that Highlander did not popularize the idea of easy decapitations in a fight; nor has VtM ever really worked in a fashion that makes it close to an optimal way to fight. As far as I can tell the primary factor has always been, &quot;since vampires don't have functional bodies like humans, what exactly is the justification for them dying for good after taking too much damage?&quot; with the answer of &quot;when the head is functionally removed, because we already made staking incapacitation over a killing blow.&quot;<br /> <br /> Highlander fights might be been a cool extra visual for the game to lean into, but the rules have never actually leaned into it as some sort of default over being what you did to kill off a vampire during the day if you don't want to use fire or sunlight.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-25T23:41:36-07:00","id":1562173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1560716-dangers-to-vampires-new-systems?p=1562164#post1562164\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don\u2019t know if this is actually backed up by the scholarship or is instead an urban legend or just-so story,</div> </div> </div> </div>Urban legend. Kennedy's assassination isn't <b><i>that</i></b> influent even in the US.<br /> <br /> Just reinforcing and maybe adding to what Heavy Arms said, the head is the most assured target to kill someone, but not to stop them. In a real combat, stopping is way more important than killing.<br /> <br /> So executions have included decapitations since before the written word. In fact they're still used, or substituted by headshots, and are definitely more humane than US inventions in the area since Edison. The reason why you don't see people calling for a return of Demoiselle Guillotine today is that those who want more humane treatments are against executions, period.<br /> <br /> Killing that way in battle is rarely a thing, but the idea of doing so has always been popular. People had unrealistic fantasies about fights in the past just as much as us.<br /> <br /> Hitting the chest is both easier and more effective at stopping the target. Not only is the area full of vitals and easier to hit, its also full of reflexes that force the person to pause. A hit to the Sternum can stop you for several precious seconds to recover wind even if the damage is minor. A shot will put you to the ground right away in a similar fashion.<br /> <br /> There is a partial exception, but with a reason: snipers are not in battle. Their whole thing is to wait for the perfect moment to land a shot that finishes the job. This isn't always the head, but it is frequently enough the head. Kennedy was just an example, not first, not last, not a change of trends. <hr /><br /> The reason I keep citing Highlander is because the books themselves do so.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying VtM does it exactly alike. It is not realistic for me, it's not very close to real life, but definitely isn't a reliable method in the game.<br /> <br /> But I dare to say that's the exact reason why the Highlander nod is strong: the rule just sits there, almost unused, because it's too hard to use. It's not really meant to do anything. At this point, the nod is the only reason for it to exist in the book at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T12:30:43-07:00","id":1562191,"title":"","content":"Also... Kennedy was struck twice, once in the upper back/neck, and then in the head (and there was the missed shot on top of that)... it wasn't some clean movie-style sniper one-shot-one-skill event.<br /> <br /> ------------------<br /> <br /> Also... where does VtM keep referencing Highlander exactly? Once in the inspirational media section long with movies like Alien and Blade Runner? Not even at all in V20? Why are we ignoring the relevance of things like Highlander dealing with concepts of trying to keep connections to humanity despite being immortal (or just tossing it away completely to become a monster), while hiding your true nature?<br /> <br /> And I've provided a pretty clear reason for the rule to exist: to define if, and how much, non-bane physical damage a vampire body can take before it is destroyed since it doesn't need to breathe or have functional internal organs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T13:10:47-07:00","id":1562194,"title":"","content":"Those are good points<br /> <br /> For the first, I don't think Highlander only influenced the beheadings. I remember some conversations being tossed around in the 2000s about how much VtM was way more Highlander than any literary vampire, and not in a deprecating way. It's more that I didn't saw a reason for this specific rule besides that, but the game is full of it, and of the best Highlander movies btw.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure about the second point, but I can run with it. As a marker of &quot;this is the bare minimum to fry one through damage alone&quot; this works. Anyway, I'll need some more weeks to post all new versions because I'm quite slow and perfectionist.<br /> <br /> Or maybe I'll post some of it later today, who knows.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-30T06:28:20-07:00","id":1562342,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:20px\"><u><b>Sunlight and Daysleep \u2013 Expanded</b></u></span><br /> <br /> As tradition dictates, Kindred are nocturnal predators with aversion to the sun and the day in general, consigned to exist fully as a society only in the dark of the night.<br /> <br /> That said, this is not as old a tradition and not something we can't tinker with. As a thematic rule, no major balance issue occur if you alter or remove it.<br /> <br /> For the record: Dracula, Carmilla, Ruthven and Varney, the four founders of the myth of the Vampire, never got harmed by the sun, they could walk in plain daylight even if they didn't much like it. 103 years separate Ruthven from Nosferatu, the first ever to be harmed (and killed) by the sun. Way more time than what went between the start of the tradition and the first Vampire to ignore it again, the Queen of the Damned.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Assumptions</b></span></span><br /> <br /> The assumptions you make about the game should guide your hand on deciding how much to make the day dangerous or not. Choose them carefully.<br /> <br /> <b>Special</b>: remember that Aggravated damage isn't just an immediate threat, it lingers for some time. Depending on game's pacing, any damage of this caliber will make things harder for the character for a while.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Current Game Standards</b></span></span><br /> <br /> 1. Sunlight is dangerous and Daysleep is incapacitating. Kindred society simply can't afford to not be exclusively nocturnal.<br /> 2. Damnation adds nuance, not all monsters are equally at danger. Those closer to mortals can afford a longer stay.<br /> 3. Indirect exposure matters. You can be tactical about it, you can take calculated risks.<br /> 4. Mortals are dangerous. A main reason is their ability to exploit the greatest of Kindred weaknesses (<i>this</i> is balance-relevant).<br /> 5. The day itself imposes the Slumber. If not completely incapacitated, vampires are sluggish at best and helpless at worse during the day, sun or not.<br /> 6. Vampires should still be able to flee the scene if endangered during the day. This is to prevent insta-kill buttons in the game.<br /> 7. Humanity is a buffer against the Slumber. The more you have, the easier to function during the day.<br /> 8. Any daily activity is a fight against the Slumber, and it will win. Only the most Humane pose a chance to stay a whole day awake.<br /> 9. Sleep isn't necessary. The Rouse Check of the night readies the vampire for the rest of it.<br /> 10. CAUTION: excess damage flattens it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\">Tropes of the Game</span></b></span><br /> <br /> 1. The sun is a dream. Any Vampire with even a modicum of capacity to actually walk in the day is both rare and target of deadly envy.<br /> 2. Walk into the sunlight is both metaphor and actual suicide method, as is &quot;<i>greeting the sun</i>&quot; a metaphor and method for execution.<br /> 3. A vampire is the ultimate sin. The sun is the ultimate cleanser. Only holiness or divine intervention protect the Damned.<br /> 4. Servitors are needed. A Vampire without mortals to get things done in the day is missing a limb.<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\">Unused Tropes</span></span></b><br /> <br /> 1. The sun is utterly lethal. No Kindred can dare to face it beyond mere seconds.<br /> 2. A vampire is harmless during the day. Even under threat to life they can't wake.<br /> 3. The sun burns sin from Vampires. The ones who avoid it can still face its rays unhindered.<br /> 4. Only direct sunlight harms Vampires. Without it they can't even tell day from night better than a mortal (classic).<br /> 5. Sunlight is a physical barrier. A Vampire isn't as much harmed by it as is trapped and unable to enter or, if surrounded, move. One caught in the sun is a corpse and nothing more until nightfall.<br /> 6. Sunlight is dangerous, but manageable. It isn't safe to take it unprotected, but a sufficiently covered Kindred can give it a try. Their society is still nocturnal, but the odd man wandering around with too much cover in the summer may be one of them (more urban legend).<br /> 7. Sunlight is a discomfort, not a danger. It dampens powers, is overall annoying, but vampires can take it. Their society is still mostly nocturnal, but it's harder to tell which of your neighbors are one of them. Maybe a whole street (original and good for some horror tropes).<br /> 8. Power staves off the danger of sunlight for good. Higher Blood Potency protect the older monsters, making them even more dangerous (Queen of the Damned style, it increases the sense of mythical might of the Methuselah).<br /> 9. Vampires don't sleep, except to recover from grievous injuries and other mystic activities. During the day the creatures are hiding in the dark places of the city, plotting and preparing for when lady Night frees them from captivity (good for some horror tropes, but also to get the time jumps out of your game.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Proposals</b></span></span><br /> <br /> The first two just simplify what's already how it works in practice. <b><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\">The</span> Mercy</b> and <span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>The Aura</b></span> are meant to work together and build something that actually follows the intent of the rules.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Daylight Simple Rule (baseline)</b></span></span><br /> <br /> A vampire suffer 2 Agg dmg each turn exposed to sunlight. The ST is the final judge on what counts as exposure, but there's no partial damage.<br /> <br /> <i>This is simpler but effectively the same as the current rules.</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Daylight Death Sentence</b></span></span><br /> <br /> A vampire has 3 rounds to go back to the darkness, turning to ashes if still exposed to the sun at the end of the timer.<br /> <br /> <i>This removes variables and any advantage from higher stats or powers, but the vampire take no damage if fast enough, good balance</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>The Mercy (Humanity as Shield)</b></span></span><br /> <br /> The purifying light of the sun punishes the Beast, not the Human beneath. If being moral protects against the sun, the rate of Aggravated damage is as follows:<br /> <br /> - <b>Humanity 0</b>: instant destruction<br /> - <b>Humanity 1-2</b>: 2/turn<br /> - <b>Humanity 3-4</b>: 1/2 turns<br /> - <b>Humanity 5-6</b>: 1/scene<br /> - <b>Humanity 7-8</b>: 1/day<br /> - <b>Humanity 9-10</b>: 1 Superficial/Day<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Commentary:</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <i>Humanity 0</i>: A Wight has no place under the sun, ever.<br /> <i>Humanity 1-2</i>: Regular, pretty harsh, run or perish.<br /> <i>Humanity 3-4</i>: This is still Agg, but you can brave a short run without a death sentence.<br /> <i>Humanity 5-6</i>: Still free Agg, but once in a while you can make a painful sacrifice to take care of business<br /> <i>Humanity 7-8</i>: Don't get confident, this will burn through your reserves (and skin) fast if you abuse, but you can actually function during the day despite the smell of bacon.<br /> <i>Humanity 9-10</i>: Daywalker. You deserve it.<br /> <br /> Most functional Vampires are between Humanity 3 and 5: they can't really afford to brave the sun outside extraordinary need, but have a chance at fleeing if just thrown there.<br /> <br /> Starting at 7 gives the PCs a taste of the day as an option, but the cost is still high: a day under the sun is another healing Agg at a cost of 3 Rouse Checks, and feeding is limited by the very fact of being Humanity 7 or 8.<br /> <br /> Vampires at enough Humanity to not be toast in seconds become both an asset and envied. They get a role in society that partially offsets the political disadvantage of being scrupulous, but many would like to bring them down eventually.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Dials:</b></span></span><br /> <br /> 1. Move every category down a peg and let Humanity 9-10 be immune. This will make for a new Kindred society where managing Humanity is a focal concern and the day is not off-limits, but mostly still stay nocturnal.<br /> <br /> 2. Move every category up a peg and let Wights explode in flames, starting fires. This leaves most vampires in the old rules or close, while the really dedicated do reap some benefit and also become too unreliable to be used regularly as an asset by others. They only get the anger.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>The Judge's Eye (The Curse as Factor)</b></span></span><br /> <br /> The stronger the Curse, the harder it is to escape judgement by the sun itself. This is about escaping notice: low Blood Potency can dare a little exposure without being \u201cfound out\u201d, while the most powerful must avoid any hint of daylight to survive.<br /> <br /> With that, this is a binary situation: a character either is under the effects of the sun or isn't. If protected, they suffer no further effects. If they are exposed, full effects apply. You can combine this rule with others for damage and/or Daysleep.<br /> <br /> <i>Exposure Score:</i><br /> <br /> Start with an Exposure Score of 8, then apply modifiers as presented in following sections. A Vampire is protected from Sunlight at Exposure 0 or below.<br /> <br /> - <b>Exposure 8</b>: Bright Daylight \u27a1\ufe0f start at this<br /> - <b>Exposure 0</b>: Safety Threshold<br /> <br /> <i>Exposure Conditions:</i><br /> <br /> - <b>Dappled Shade -2</b><br /> ---- Tree canopy or translucent shade<br /> <br /> - <b>Light Cloud Cover -4</b><br /> ---- Any shade that still has shadows<br /> <br /> - <b>Heavy Cloud Cover -6</b><br /> ---- Heavy enough to have no shadow<br /> <br /> - <b>Veiled Indoors -8</b><br /> ---- Curtains or other barrier for light<br /> <br /> - <b>Deep Indoors -10</b><br /> ---- No direct light, visibly dimmer<br /> <br /> - <b>Adjusted Darkness -12</b><br /> ---- Shapes visible, details obscured<br /> <br /> - <b>Absolute Darkness -14</b><br /> ---- Found in the depths of the world<br /> <br /> <i>Protective Gear:</i><br /> <br /> - <b>Minimal -1</b><br /> ---- Long sleeves, hat or sunglasses<br /> ---- Not weird, but easy to remember<br /> - <b>Light -2</b><br /> ---- Long coat, hat, glasses, gloves<br /> ---- In a hot day or place you get stares<br /> - <b>Moderate -3</b><br /> ---- Add large trenchcoat and scarf<br /> ---- It's either winter or you're weird<br /> - <b>Heavy -4</b><br /> ---- Add mask and prescription glasses<br /> ---- Your aversion to light is undeniable<br /> - <b>Maximum -6</b><br /> ---- Full-body suit and closed helmet<br /> ---- This is specialized &amp; unmistakable<br /> - <b>Dark Armor -9</b><br /> ---- That's at least a space suit<br /> ---- You're a comic book character<br /> <br /> <i>Curse Effect:</i><br /> <br /> - Add Bane Severity to Exposure.<br /> - If Exposure 1+, apply sunlight.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Commentary:</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Thin Blood (BS 0)</b>: in any overcast day they can try to walk around if they have the right gear. Otherwise, avoiding direct light is safe enough for them.<br /> <br /> <b>Child/Neonate (BS 2)</b>: they can brave the streets in the cloudiest days, and sleep anywhere without a non-blocked window.<br /> <br /> <b>Ancillae (BS 3)</b>: with adequate clothes they can wander sunlit rooms as long as they remain indoors, away from open windows.<br /> <br /> <b>Elder (BS 4)</b>: rooms with windows already pose a risk without good preparation, they feel truly safe only in the dark.<br /> <br /> <b>Greater Elders (BS 5)</b>: darkness is their domain, even if they can try a dimly lit room with effort. It's unlikely they'll want to.<br /> <br /> <b>Methuselah (BS 6)</b>: preparation can ease things, but safety is only obtained in the darkest pits. A coffin is for real a must have.<br /> <br /> This adds a layer of risk management and strategy to Kindred relationship with light. It is something that will permeate the society:<br /> <br /> - Vampires in need can operate during the day without being consigned only to catacombs and sealed havens. While the amount of freedom varies, most can now manage <i>something</i>.<br /> - Neonates are an even greater asset and a strong practical reason for Embraces, as new Vampires can navigate the day to a greater extent.<br /> - Old monsters still reced to the darkness as they see little reason to exert as little freedom at such a risk. Stratification in vampiric society becomes more visible as they literally occupy different places.<br /> - It is a risk. Miscalculation or accidents can put you in a higher exposure level than planned and end up frying you. It's not a decision to be taken lightly, but to be carefully planned ahead.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Dials:</b></span></span><br /> <br /> 1. Widen the gap using the tables below and adding <i><b>Blood Potency</b></i> instead of <i><b>Bane Severity</b></i>. This will lock each one more rigidly in their functional niches and increase the penalty for high BP at the same time that increasing problems feel more gradual.<br /> 2. Give <i><b>Thin-Bloods</b></i> a -2 bonus. The Thin-Blood Advantage <i>Day Drinker</i> has no penalties anymore. A well prepared Thin-Blood can make do with umbrellas and Day Drinkers really can walk at day.<br /> 3. Use the <i>Day Drinker</i> penalties for those at Exposure 1 instead of damage. That way Vampires can give up some power for slightly more mobility. It goes a long way.<br /> 4. Use the <i>Day Drinker</i> penalties for those at Exposure 0 instead of protection. At the threshold vampires pay a cost to function at all, the benefits of this rule are sensibly reduced in practice.<br /> <br /> <i>Wider Gap Exposure Score:</i><br /> <br /> - <b>Exposure 11</b>: Bright Daylight<br /> - <b>Exposure 0</b>: Safety Threshold<br /> <br /> <i>Wider Gap Exposure Conditions:</i><br /> <br /> - <b>Dappled Shade -3</b><br /> - <b>Light Cloud Cover -6</b><br /> - <b>Heavy Cloud Cover -9</b><br /> - <b>Veiled Indoors -12</b><br /> - <b>Deep Indoors -15</b><br /> - <b>Adjusted Darkness -18</b><br /> - <b>Absolute Darkness -21</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Daysleep Bonus Dice</b></span></span><br /> <br /> You can have all sorts of sources for bonus dice on the roll to awake or remain awake. It is interesting to think why it affects the Curse in such a particular way. You should expect PCs to seek those bonuses and spend more time awake during the day simply by virtue of being reminded they can.<br /> <br /> - <b>Minor Benefit +1</b><br /> ---- Almost symbolic, recognizes an effect without committing to it. A Kindred can still stack those in their favor.<br /> <br /> - <b>Consistent Improvement +2</b><br /> ---- That's an extra average success. It's not a game changer, but increases the average stimulus category the Kindred can respond to. For <b>Humanity 1</b>, make success without cost possible at all.<br /> <br /> - <b>Major Benefit +3</b><br /> ---- A single source this size is serious business. <b>Humanity 7</b> Vampires can expect to wake up to minor problems more often than not, and <b>Humanity 3</b> have a similar chance to wake up if their life is threatened.<br /> <br /> - <b>Regular Success +4</b><br /> ---- Two extra average successes makes the sting of the rule sensibly lower, but still there. Starting Humanity vampires can wake up to solve problems, most can deal with emergencies if needed.<br /> <br /> - <b>Maximum Benefit +5</b><br /> ---- No single source should give all that at once. This is enough to make any vampire respond to fire threats more often than not, while starting vampires can really wake up consistently if they need.<br /> <br /> - <b>Regularly Awaken +6 +</b><br /> ---- Average successes now answer for a category of stimulus by themselves, leaving the vampire to account just for reliability and further categories.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Sample Bonuses:</b></span></span><br /> <br /> - <b>Chronicle Default +1 to +5</b><br /> ---- This establishes a new baseline for how easily/frequently vampires stay awake during the day to focus on their plots and intimate affairs.<br /> <br /> - <b>Attuned lair +1 to +5</b><br /> ---- May even be a new Background, the Attunement makes the lord of the Haven more active inside it for their own projects. It may extend to guests as an extra.<br /> <br /> - <b>No Feeding in previous night +2</b><br /> ---- Maybe it's the hungry Beast trying to find prey at any cost, maybe it's just the Human beneath finding a little respite, but it works.<br /> <br /> - <b>Caffeinated blood +1</b><br /> ---- Stimulants are drugs, too. A victim under high doses may help fight off the Daysleep, on top of other effects it may have.<br /> <br /> - <b>Touchstone is in the scene +3</b><br /> ---- Spending a day with a Touchstone or observing them is a powerful motif in the life of the undead.<br /> <br /> - <b>Hunger Level</b><br /> ---- This is a powerful motivator. With the Hunger level applied directly as a bonus, you'll nudge players to see the day as a desperate but valid opportunity for feeding. You'll also give them a strategic reason to risk staying at higher Hunger. Finally these are Hunger Dice, so expect the day to be ruled by Compulsion frequently.<br /> <br /> <i>Avoid penalties. Their most probable result will just be the PCs not trying as resisting Daysleep is hard enough to be worth the trouble.</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Daysleep Difficulty Modifier</b></span></span><br /> <br /> A few sources can alter how hard it is to wake up in this manner. They differ from bonus dice in that you can't stack them to go lower than difficulty 2, although you can stack them to have an easier time waking to lighter causes.<br /> <br /> - <b>Easy Danger Roll Diff -1</b><br /> ---- Waking is a little easier, if still not guaranteed. High Humanity Vampires are reasonably safe against fire, while Humanity 4 can now escape burning to death more often than not.<br /> <br /> - <b>Easy Important Roll Diff -2</b><br /> ---- Vampires wake for serious matters at least as easily as for fire. This change their potential relationship with their mortal servants, as now they can be called on important matters. For PCs it means you can have the characters participating in important day events without contrived plots or much luck.<br /> <br /> - <b>Easy Inconvenience Roll Diff -3</b><br /> ---- Baseline now let Vampires wake for minor problems \u2013 that may just be not so minor, as in hunters approaching silently. Waking up just because they want is now as easy as staying awake after that.<br /> <br /> - <b>Easy Interest Diff -4</b><br /> ---- Awaking is Diff 2 for whatever reason. While many Vampires won't even try, the creatures regularly stay up during the day. Mortals still have the upper hand of numbers and not frying under the sun, but the undead plot their big conspiracies all the time, and the dark places are dangerous.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Sample Reductions</b></span></span><br /> - <b>Chronicle Default -1 to -4</b><br /> ---- This establishes a new baseline for how easily/frequently vampires stay awake during the day to focus on their plots and intimate affairs.<br /> <br /> - <b>Alarm Setup -1 to -2</b><br /> ---- A few clocks or a servant that do muster the courage to shake the vampire awake is -1. A system of stimuli made to wake up corpses may manage just that.<br /> <br /> - <b>Neighborhood Resonance -1 to -4</b><br /> ---- While this is not necessarily the same as Blood Resonance, it may be related. The important thing is that some areas in the city can be easier for some Kindred to stay awake when there.<br /> <br /> - <b>Supernatural Beacon -1 to -4</b><br /> ---- Powerful effects may rouse the Kindred into vigilance all day long in an area. Powerful presences, special magics, holes into the spirit world.<br /> <br /> <b>Special</b>: you can decide Diff can go as low as 1. This is going to make waking up way easier and more common, but isn't bad by itself. Do consider the rule <i><b>Absolutes</b></i>, though.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Winning At A Cost - Day Awakening</b></span></span><br /> <br /> Standardizing the cost to cover remaining successes is a great opportunity to make it a more accessible option and, at the same time, define effects that reinforce themes you want.<br /> <br /> - <b>Superficial Willpower Damage</b><br /> ---- Each success costs a Willpower point. Simple, direct, cut on resource, that's it.<br /> <br /> - <b>Rouse Check</b><br /> ---- This is a heft cost, it should be either a single Check no matter how many are needed, or a Check for 2 successes. The Rouse Check of the night will still happen as normal.<br /> <br /> - <b>Waking Sickness</b><br /> ---- The character starts further capped in a number of dice equal to the lacking successes. Each relevant roll under this penalty reduces it in 1.<br /> <br /> - <b>Memory of Deprivation</b><br /> ---- The shock brings back one piece of memory from their living days: what it feels like to lack sleep. The sensation causes Impairment until nightfall, and then an additional scene per success needed.<br /> <br /> - <b>Subsumed Beast</b><br /> ---- The Beast doesn't wake as completely and this is bad, because it houses the curse that empowers the Vampire. For each success short of the Diff, the player gives up one trait completely until they sleep a full day again. A whole Discipline is the standard, but they may chose <i><b>Mend</b></i> or <i><b>Blood Surge</b></i>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Day Awakening Duration</b></span></span><br /> <br /> Regular rule is to give the character a full scene, then ask for a new roll each new scene. That's reasonable, but too easy for a game that want to limit day action severely without cutting fire defense altogether, and too cumbersome if players want to engage in something all day long.<br /> <br /> - Give the Awakening roll\u2019s margin as extra scenes before the next roll. That will reduce the need for repeating rolls all day long.<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Dials:</span></span></b><br /> <br /> - Change from roll per scene to roll per turn and margins to extra turns. You may want to also reduce the Difficulty of Awakening rolls as this will severely limit the reliability of awakening in the day, and too many rolls will be a chore.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Sleep Benefits</b></span></span><br /> <br /> Those reinforce the theme of the Daysleep by giving an actual advantage on pursuing it. This makes the choice of staying awake vs undergoing the Daysleep more strategical.<br /> <br /> You get the benefit only after a full day of slumber, from sunrise to sunset.<br /> <br /> - <b>Willpower Healing</b><br /> ---- Heal one superficial willpower damage<br /> <br /> - <b>Resonance Attuning</b><br /> ---- Increase your current Resonance by 1 level of Intensity. If it would give you a Dyscrasia, the effect is: \u201cyou know if someone in your vicinity has the same Resonance. Consume the Dyscrasia to pinpoint them\u201d.<br /> <br /> - <b>The Blush of Life</b><br /> ---- Upon awakening, activating the Blush of Life let you roll two dice and keep the better.<br /> <br /> - <b>Shackle the Beast</b><br /> ---- You get +1 dice on the very next roll to resist Frenzy.<br /> <br /> - <b>Memoriam Dice</b><br /> ---- The character keeps all benefits they met through the night: if they stood at the place of the memory, drank blood of a witness and held the artifact, then it's as if they were all at the same time and add an extra dice for the sleep.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Sleep Deprivation</b></span></span><br /> <br /> The opposite of the previous, this will tie the Daysleep to basic functions of undead flesh and blood, or otherwise reduce the character as the Curse claims the price.<br /> <br /> Any interruption of sleep, delay to start or a too early awakening kicks in the penalties, and they stay until the vampire sleeps a full day.<br /> <br /> - <b>Locked Reflexes</b><br /> ---- The physiology of the undead isn't fully responsive. The Kindred can't use their fangs, the Blush of Life or Blood Surge. They all still exist as functions and can activate suddenly, no cost, at ST discretion, but are outside control.<br /> <br /> - <b>Dead Flesh</b><br /> ---- Similar to the <i><b>Thin Blood Flaw</b></i>, you're a slightly rotting corpse. Just looking at you tells you're not well, and the smell isn't any pleasant. Use those rules, but add that you're still recognizable, and people may ask questions about this malady. Beware carrion feeders, most won't bother a corpse still moving, but some are brave or dumb enough.<br /> <br /> - <b>Monstrous Hunger</b><br /> ---- Blood Potency is considered 2 levels higher for the purpose of feeding. If you're at BP 10, you can only slake your Hunger killing a human.<br /> <br /> - <b>Bestial Lability</b><br /> ---- Double your Hunger Dice.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Georgia\"><b>Absolutes</b></span></span><br /> <br /> Sometimes it's easier to just skip rolls and bookkeeping altogether. Don't shy from that if you have decided on what you want from the idea of Vampires awake at day, just do it and ignore the dice.<br /> <br /> You can decide, for example:<br /> <br /> - <b>Vampires just can do it</b><br /> ---- Staying awake, hiding from mortals is common, vampires plot all the time, sleep is for benefit/penalty or an affectation.<br /> <br /> - <b>Vampires just can't do it</b><br /> ---- If caught unguarded by day they're just screwed. This is, indeed, the most vulnerable they get, and only the most trusted servants get access to their Master's resting place.<br /> <br /> - <b>At X Humanity they do it</b><br /> ---- That gives a strong moral theme, and a hard benefit for staying above your line. 7 will distinguish starting players. 6 distinguishes the two halves of the score, 4 and 5 start to allow a majority of Vampires, while 2 and 3 make sleep a mark of final stages of degeneration.<br /> <br /> - <b>At X awakening dice they do it</b><br /> ---- Similar to above, but bonus dice exist and count towards this goal. Bonuses then mark methods that reliably work to wakeup a Vampire. If you use this, a source of Diff reduction can work as a bonus at double the value.<br /> <br /> - <b>If Hunger is 0, they can do it</b><br /> ---- Managing Hunger now has an extra benefit, and a reason for characters to seek the slaking of the last dot.<br /> <br /> - <b>Pay the price and do it</b><br /> ---- Choose one cost for it: Minor/Major<br /> -------- A Rouse Check/2 Rouse Checks<br /> -------- A Willpower point/Aggravated<br /> -------- Any Resonance/Any Dyscrasia","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}